    description: "Matt Haig's The Midnight Library opens with Nora Seed at 35, who has given up — on her swimming career, her music, her relationships, her brother, her cat, and finally her life. In the space between ending and ended, she finds the Midnight Library: a library where every book is a life she could have lived if she had made different choices. Her librarian from school is there. She can try the Olympic career, the partner she left, the research station in Antarctica. The novel asks what we actually need to be content — and whether the life we have, with its wrong turns, is the one we would choose if we could see it clearly. If the premise, the emotional honesty about mental health, and the optimism about the value of particular lives gripped you, these eight picks go to the same place.",
