    description: "Henry James's most perfectly concentrated novella (1888) — an American editor's obsessive and deceitful pursuit of the love letters of the fictional poet Jeffrey Aspern from the aged Juliana Bordereau in a crumbling Venetian palazzo, culminating in the ironic twist that destroys his moral credit and his prize simultaneously. These recommendations extend the experience through James's other major works, through the tradition of the Venice literary narrative, and through fiction that engages the ethics of the artistic pursuit.",
