    description: "Tess of the d'Urbervilles is one of the most morally serious novels in English — Hardy's argument, in his subtitle 'A Pure Woman Faithfully Presented,' that society's condemnation of Tess is wrong and that the novel will not pretend otherwise. If Hardy's indignation and his Wessex landscape moved you, these books share his portrait of women destroyed by societies that make the rules and punish those who violate them.",
